ZERO / EZERO 0.9.0 FOR WINDOWS X64

This distribution contains original Zero and Ezero compilers, both written in
Zero. All generated executables are native PE64 console applications. They
use Windows KERNEL32 services directly and need no Linux, Wine, C runtime,
Python, external assembler, foreign compiler, or installed language runtime.
The .cmd files use the Windows command interpreter to connect these programs.

Target: Windows 10/11 x64. Windows on ARM, 32-bit Windows and native Windows
execution have not yet been verified. Compatibility tests use Wine 10 on a
Debian 13 host; a Wine pass does not claim a native Windows machine test.

GET STARTED

Extract the complete directory. Open Command Prompt (cmd.exe) in it:

    call ezero.cmd examples\banana.ezero banana.exe
    banana.exe

    call ezero-run.cmd examples\functions.ezero

The banana example prints:
    3 банана
    5
    банана
    6

Source is UTF-8. For UTF-8 terminal display, use `chcp 65001` in Command Prompt
and a font supporting your characters. Redirected program output is always
binary UTF-8 with LF newlines, with no Windows text conversion.

For low-level Zero (source from stdin, executable to stdout):

    call zero.cmd < example.zero > example.exe
    example.exe

zero.cmd selects --windows by default. Pass an explicit target to cross-compile:

    call zero.cmd --host < example.zero > example-linux

Available explicit targets: --windows, --host, --raw, --raw32, --efi, --efi32,
--real, --app64 and --app32. The app targets require Zero OS's own application
ABI; they do not produce Windows executables.

The internal bin\zero.exe has the shared cross-platform compiler defaults;
when invoking it directly, always select --windows for Windows output:

    bin\zero.exe --windows < example.zero > example.exe

Use Command Prompt for these binary redirections. Older Windows PowerShell
versions may transform redirected binary bytes; their redirection is not part
of this package. Ezero's wrapper handles binary files with Windows copy /b.

REBUILD ON WINDOWS

    call rebuild.cmd

This compiles the included Zero compiler source twice using Zero, compares the
two PE files byte for byte with the included original Zero checker, and builds
Ezero's original frontend and packer.
The source and both runtime fragments are included. No downloaded sources or
third-party language implementation is included. An existing Zero executable
is the bootstrap seed for this self-hosting cycle.

EZERO

Ezero is the easy, high-level language built on Zero. It supports automatic
value kinds, unquoted UTF-8 text, typed phrases such as `3 банана`, numbers,
lists, one-based collection indices, loops, functions, recursion and automatic
memory collection. See EZERO-LANGUAGE.ztext and EZERO-MEMORY.ztext for the precise
syntax, limits and behavior. Programs run on the original Ezero runtime built
into each executable by the original Zero compiler.

`ezero INPUT.ezero OUTPUT.exe` compiles before replacing the destination; a
syntax error preserves an existing executable. It rejects identical source
and output paths. `ezero-run INPUT.ezero` builds in a temporary directory,
runs the executable, preserves its exit status and removes its temporary files.

WINDOWS HOST PROFILE

Supported Zero primitives: put, exit, store8/16/32/64, load8/16/32/64,
reserve, read, write, bitxor, bitand, shr, fork and switch.
The existing compiler rejects raw hardware/firmware and Unix positioned-file
primitives in Windows mode. Fork/switch are Zero cooperative contexts within
one process, not Windows processes or threads. Stack/arena layout follows the
same original Zero 4 MiB hosted memory profile. Each PE requests a committed
16 MiB stack to reserve the arena without borrowing a CRT stack probe.

read/write use ReadFile/WriteFile on the standard handles. Count must be at
most 0x7fffffff; a larger count returns unsigned -22. EOF returns0. Other I/O
failures return unsigned -5. Short reads/writes remain visible to the caller.
put exits74 on a failed one-byte write. Exit statuses use the low eight bits.
The compiler emits no output until parsing and finalization succeed.

The native startup reconstructs the compiler's existing argc/argv layout.
Its bounded command-line profile allows at most4095 bytes and63 arguments;
spaces outside double quotes separate arguments. Double quotes group paths
with spaces. Unclosed quotes and oversized command lines exit64. It does not
implement the complete Windows CRT backslash-before-quote grammar; compiler
options contain no such quoting. Source bytes arrive through binary stdin.

IMPLEMENTATION / VALIDATION

The PE header, import table, relocation padding and x64 instructions are
emitted by original Zero code. No external assembler/linker/PE library is
used. The sole imported library is the host operating system's KERNEL32.dll.
All references are position-independent; .text is executable/read-only,
.idata writable/non-executable, and .reloc discardable/non-executable.

Release 0.9.0 verification: all 85 Ezero behavioral cases, 25 native ABI/PE
checks and 12 Windows wrapper/checker cases passed under Wine. The final
merged Zero compiler reproduced its Windows executable byte for byte.

Verification records live in the development tree under
verification/windows-0.9.0 and portability/windows/STATUS.ztext.
External Wine, Python, shell and binary-inspection tools only orchestrate tests
or package files; none are required by or included in the Windows product.
